```cs

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_RemoveContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            arrList.Remove(x);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount <= Old_arrListCount); // new array list has size at most as much as old array list

        }
    }
}

```
--------------------```cs
Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount <= Old_arrListCount);

// @@@ after removing, new array list doesn't contain the old x value 

Debug.Assert(!New_arrListContainsX);

// @@@ new array list doesn't contain the old value of x, and old x isn't in new list 

Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX == -1 && Old_arrListLastIndexOfX == -1);

// @@@ index of x in old array list gives -1 in the old list, and in new list same value gives -1 

Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX == -1 && Old_arrListIndexOfX == -1);
```
--------------------Here are the Debug.Assert statements corresponding to the comments that start with "@@@":

```cs
Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount <= Old_arrListCount, "new array list has size at most as much as old array list");
```

Just make sure to include the following line at the beginning of your C# code file to allow usage of Debug.Assert:

```cs
using System.Diagnostics;
```
--------------------```cs
Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount <= Old_arrListCount);
```
This assert statement checks that the new size of the array list (i.e., the number of elements in the list after a Remove operation) is no more than the old size of the array list (i.e., the number of elements before the Remove operation). This makes sense as the Remove operation should at most reduce the size of the array list by one, but it could also be zero in some edge cases.
--------------------Here are the debug.assert statements corresponding to the comments:

```cs
Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount <= Old_arrListCount);
```

This statement asserts that the new ArrayList count should be less than or equal to the old ArrayList count, meaning the new ArrayList size is at most the same as the old ArrayList size after the removal operation. If this condition is met, it means the removal was successful, otherwise, it indicates a problem.
--------------------Here are the Debug.Assert statements corresponding to the comments that start with "@@@":

```cs
Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount <= Old_arrListCount, "new array list has size at most as much as old array list");
```

This assertion checks whether the 'new' (post-removal) size of the array list is less than or equal to the 'old' (pre-removal) size of the array list. If the 'new' size is greater than the 'old' size, the assertion will fail, indicating an issue in the code where the list's size is not being properly updated after a remove operation.
--------------------Here is the corresponding C# code that uses "Debug.Assert" to match the comments:

```cs
Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount <= Old_arrListCount);

// @@@ After removal, old array list has count one less than before 

// @@@ array list size has changed by at least 1
Debug.Assert(Math.Abs(Old_arrListCount - New_arrListCount) >= 1);

// @@@ the element to be removed is in the array list
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListContainsX == true);

// @@@ after removal, the array list doesn't contain the element anymore
Debug.Assert(New_arrListContainsX == false);

// @@@ after removal, index of element in the array list is not found
Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX == -1);

// @@@ last index of element in the array list before removal is equal to new index of element after removal
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX == New_arrListLastIndexOfX);

// @@@ after removal, count of elements in array list is less than or equal to count before removal
Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount <= Old_arrListCount);
```

These assertions are based on the specific actions performed in the `PUT_RemoveContract` method which would verify the contract of the `ArrayList` class for the `Remove` method. Each assertion checks a specific precondition or postcondition for the `Remove` method.
